1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,800
Welcome to Bellevue Museum, and
thank you for picking up our audio guide.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:06,856
Many visitors assume
the museum dates back to

3
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,392
the 1890s, when the
idea was first proposed,

4
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:15,600
but it didn't actually open its doors until
1901. A major extension followed in 1925.

5
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:18,567
The building itself
has an interesting past.

6
00:00:18,591 --> 00:00:21,207
It looks so grand that
people often guess it was

7
00:00:21,208 --> 00:00:24,025
once a bank or a school,
but for most of the 19th

8
00:00:24,049 --> 00:00:27,117
century it stored goods
unloaded at the nearby docks.

9
00:00:27,118 --> 00:00:29,555
Look up, and you
can still see the iron

10
00:00:29,579 --> 00:00:32,626
beams of the old warehouse.
When the museum first

11
00:00:32,627 --> 00:00:34,625
opened, there were
just six galleries.

12
00:00:34,649 --> 00:00:37,747
Two more were added later,
which gives us the eight we have

13
00:00:37,748 --> 00:00:41,293
today, although we hope to
reach ten within the next few years.

14
00:00:41,317 --> 00:00:45,596
Our natural history rooms are much loved,
and the modern art gallery gets plenty

15
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:48,067
of attention, but the
crowds are always biggest

16
00:00:48,068 --> 00:00:50,205
around the mummies
in the Egyptian gallery.

17
00:00:50,229 --> 00:00:52,772
There was a lot of debate
about our newest gallery.

18
00:00:53,259 --> 00:00:55,854
The public voted for
space exploration, and our

19
00:00:55,878 --> 00:00:58,652
children's panel wanted
dinosaurs. But in the end,

20
00:00:58,705 --> 00:01:03,380
the trustees chose to tell the story of the
town itself, from fishing village to city.

21
00:01:03,779 --> 00:01:05,962
Before you set off, the
shop is worth a look.

22
00:01:05,986 --> 00:01:08,607
It used to be in the basement,
but it has now moved to

23
00:01:08,608 --> 00:01:10,901
the ground floor,
beside the main entrance.

24
00:01:10,925 --> 00:01:14,067
This tour has been designed
to last an hour. You could rush

25
00:01:14,068 --> 00:01:17,326
around the highlights in 45 minutes,
but we wouldn't recommend it.

26
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:19,107
You're welcome to
take photographs,

27
00:01:19,108 --> 00:01:21,882
and there are benches in
every gallery if you need a rest.

28
00:01:21,906 --> 00:01:23,992
The one thing we ask
is that you switch off

29
00:01:24,029 --> 00:01:26,658
your flash, as strong light
damages older objects.

30
00:01:26,682 --> 00:01:29,107
If you're visiting with
children, head for the

31
00:01:29,108 --> 00:01:31,688
natural history gallery,
where young visitors can

32
00:01:31,712 --> 00:01:34,307
touch real fossils. The
building stays open until

33
00:01:34,308 --> 00:01:39,025
six for the cafe and shop, but the galleries
themselves close half an hour earlier.

34
00:01:39,026 --> 00:01:42,526
Our special exhibition changes
four times a year, so there's always

35
00:01:42,550 --> 00:01:45,272
something new to see.
Students receive a quarter off

36
00:01:45,359 --> 00:01:50,140
the normal ticket price on showing a valid
card, and children under five go free.

37
00:01:50,141 --> 00:01:52,344
Many visitors expect
to find the cafe near the

38
00:01:52,368 --> 00:01:54,627
entrance, but it's
actually right at the top of

39
00:01:54,628 --> 00:01:56,883
the building, with
views over the town.

40
00:01:56,907 --> 00:01:59,570
In summer, there's also
a small kiosk outside.

41
00:01:59,571 --> 00:02:02,218
People often ask about
our most valuable item.

42
00:02:02,242 --> 00:02:05,987
Our medieval manuscript is very
rare, and the bronze sculpture in

43
00:02:05,988 --> 00:02:08,930
the hall is ensured for a
huge sum, but the single

44
00:02:08,954 --> 00:02:12,495
most valuable object we own
is the portrait in gallery four.

45
00:02:12,583 --> 00:02:15,051
The Egyptian collection
was mostly gathered

46
00:02:15,075 --> 00:02:18,072
by Victorian collectors in
the 19th century, but the

47
00:02:18,073 --> 00:02:21,423
objects themselves are
far older. Nearly all of them

48
00:02:21,447 --> 00:02:24,952
date from ancient times. We
have around 20 volunteers,

49
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:27,418
and of our paid staff,
12 workers' guides.

50
00:02:27,442 --> 00:02:31,209
As you leave the entrance hall,
take a look at the main staircase.

51
00:02:31,210 --> 00:02:36,790
It's often mistaken for stone, but it's
actually marble. Only the handrail is wood.

52
00:02:36,878 --> 00:02:39,722
Teachers, please note that
school groups shouldn't wait at

53
00:02:39,746 --> 00:02:42,227
the main entrance or in
the cafe, but go straight to

54
00:02:42,228 --> 00:02:44,911
the education room. If
you prefer a calm visit,

55
00:02:44,935 --> 00:02:48,718
avoid lunchtime and late afternoon
when the coach parties arrive.

56
00:02:48,719 --> 00:02:51,295
Mornings are by
far the quietest time.

57
00:02:51,319 --> 00:02:55,251
And finally, entry is free on
the first Tuesday of every month.

58
00:02:55,252 --> 00:02:57,208
We're also open
on public holidays,

59
00:02:57,232 --> 00:03:01,180
but full prices apply. Enjoy the
rest of your visit to Bellevue Museum.

